--TEST--
A sink carrier reached through an operand whose static type names no class renders as exactly the bytes it carries, agreeing with the declared binding rather than asking for a `toString` it has none of — `rule:security/capture-answers-the-carrier`, `rule:types/erased-member-access`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// `nvs_stdlib::registry::class_renders` has two rows and this is the second:
// a carrier is the sink's own value type, holding bytes that have *already*
// been through the sink, so it renders as precisely those bytes and asks for
// no member at all. The first row — a `Core` class the spec gives a
// `toString` — is the sibling case
// `a-core-object-behind-an-erased-operand-renders-through-the-same-member`,
// and the two rows reaching two different renderings is what keeps a carrier
// from being put through its own sink twice.
//
// `rule:types/erased-member-access` is why the erased spelling has to answer at all: a `mixed`
// defers which class is behind the handle to the concrete instance, so the
// rendering is decided at run time and cannot be refused where it is written.
//
// `Core\Html\Markup` is the other carrier and is not reachable from source
// yet — `Core\Html` arrives at M7 — so `Core\Cli\Text` is the whole of what
// this can ask today.
class Carried {
    // Every rendering spelling there is, over one `mixed`, counted against the
    // same carrier read through its own declared type.
    public static function agrees(mixed $m, Core\Cli\Text $t): int {
        string $direct = $t as string;
        int $agree = 0;
        if ("$m" == $direct) { $agree = $agree + 1; }
        if (("" . $m) == $direct) { $agree = $agree + 1; }
        if (($m as string) == $direct) { $agree = $agree + 1; }
        string $echoed = Core\Out::capture(fn (): void => { echo $m; }) as string;
        if ($echoed == $direct) { $agree = $agree + 1; }
        return $agree;
    }
}

// Bytes a sink applied twice would have had a second go at: `<`, `&` and a
// newline all survive as themselves, because the capture already put them
// through the CLI sink once.
Core\Cli\Text $t = Core\Out::capture(fn (): void => {
    echo "a<b&c";
    echo "\n";
    echo "d";
}) as Core\Cli\Text;
mixed $m = $t;

echo Carried::agrees($m, $t), "\n";
echo $m, "\n";
echo "[" . $m . "]\n";

// A carrier with nothing in it is an empty rendering, not a `null` and not the
// class name — the boundary the roster's other row answers with a throw.
mixed $empty = Core\Out::capture(fn (): void => {});
echo "[", $empty, "]\n";
--EXPECT--
4
a<b&c
d
[a<b&c
d]
[]
